---
title: Noise
---
Smooth random values from −1 to 1 at every point, most often read as altitude to make an overworld [[t:149-151 t:162-164]].

## The rule
1. For every tile, sample fractal noise at the tile’s position [[c11:20-30]].
2. Read the value by mode:

{% table label="What a tile becomes, by mode" rowheads=true %}
* Mode
* A tile becomes
---
* `height` (c10)
* gray, as bright as its value brought into 0 to 1 [[c10:21-24]]
---
* `overworld` (c11)
* water below 0, grass from 0 up to 0.5, mountain from 0.5 [[c11:31-44]]
---
* `zoom` (c12)
* the same three bands, with every sample position multiplied by a scale that shrinks 0.01 per frame [[c12:31-47 c12:77-91]]
---
* `double` (c13)
* the zoom, with a second noise mixed in by weights that change with the scale [[c13:39-44 c13:150-164]]
{% /table %}

- **Simplex, not Perlin:** the demos use fractal simplex noise, the first one too, though it is named for Perlin [[c10:13-15 c11:20-22]].
- **Talk says:** altitudes from 0 to 0.75 are green [[t:164-165]].
- **Demo:** draws mountain from 0.5. Its 0.75 is only in the water’s color [[c11:28-44]].

## Step-through
@@steps limit=8 mid=3

## Controls
@@control mode
What is made of the noise. `height`: a map in gray. `overworld`: water, grass and mountain. `zoom`: the same bands at a shrinking scale. `double`: the zoom with two noises mixed [[c10:21-26 c11:28-44 c12:31-47 c12:77-91 c13:39-44]].
@@control octaves
Talk says: how many different gradients it is mixing in [[t:154]].
@@control gain
Talk says: how long the various gradients last [[t:155]]. In our noise: each further layer’s strength is the one before it times this [[own]].
@@control lacunarity
Talk says: it adds in randomness [[t:156-158]]. In our noise: each further layer’s frequency is the one before it times this [[own]].
@@control frequency
Talk says: how frequently each of the octaves peaks [[t:159]].

## Measured traits
@@measured

- **Overworld:** its land is not one piece. {{c11.regions.median}} regions at the median, with water between them [[run:c11.regions.median]].
- **Its mix, at the median:** {{c11.water.median}} water, {{c11.grass.median}} grass, {{c11.mountain.median}} mountain [[run:c11.water.median c11.grass.median c11.mountain.median]].
- **Zoom:** its last frame is a small window of the noise, and what it holds varies widely. Floor share: {{c12.floor_share.min}} to {{c12.floor_share.max}} over the seeds [[run:c12.floor_share.min c12.floor_share.max]].
- **Height map:** all floor, so its numbers say only how big the map is [[own]].

## Failures
@@fails

- **Caveat:** our criteria are written for maps to be walked across. An overworld isn’t one until something decides where a traveler can go [[own]].

## Combinations
@@combines

- **More noise:** in the questions after the talk, its author suggests mixing several noise maps together [[t:307-314]].
- **Erosion:** its author also suggests running an erosion simulation over the noise [[t:322-324]].

